{0 The workero CLI}

[workero] turns an OCaml executable with [(modes wasm)] into a directory that
Wrangler can serve and deploy. [workero build] runs dune first.
[workero package] works on output that dune already built.

{1 Usage}

{@sh[
workero build <dir>/<name> [--out <dir>] [-- <dune args>...]
workero package <path>/<name> [--out <dir>]
]}

[--out] sets the output directory. The default is [_worker]. It can come before
or after the target. Both commands also take [--allow-missing-primitives].

{2 build}

[workero build src/main] builds [src/main.ml]. Run it from any directory inside
the dune project. [<dir>/<name>] is relative to the current directory, as it is
for [dune build].

Arguments after [--] go to [dune build]:

{@sh[
workero build src/main -- --profile dev --display short
]}

It runs [dune build <dune args> <dir>/<name>.bc.wasm.js]. It adds
[--profile release] unless the dune arguments select a profile with
[--profile], [--release] or [-p], or [DUNE_PROFILE] is set.

{2 package}

[workero package _build/default/src/main] reads
[_build/default/src/main.bc.wasm.js] and
[_build/default/src/main.bc.wasm.assets] and never runs dune. Use it when
another step builds the executable, such as a Makefile or CI job.

{1 Where it finds the build output}

[workero build] finds the output the way dune does, so it works when the
project is inside another dune project, when you run it from a subdirectory,
and when you move the build directory.

+ The dune root is the [--root] directory in the dune arguments, or
  [DUNE_ROOT]. With [--release] or [-p] it is the current directory. Otherwise
  it is the outermost parent directory of the current directory that has a
  [dune-workspace] file. If there is none, it is the outermost parent that has
  a [dune-project] file. If there is none, it is the current directory.
+ The build directory is [--build-dir] from the dune arguments, or
  [DUNE_BUILD_DIR], or [_build]. A relative path is relative to the dune root.
+ The output is in [<build directory>/default/<path of the target from the
  root>].

With an explicit root ([--root] or [DUNE_ROOT]), dune reads the target relative
to that root, not to the current directory, and so does [workero build].

{1 What it does}

+ [workero build] only: builds the loader with dune.
+ Removes the [*.wasm.assets] directories that earlier runs left in the output
  directory, so a second run leaves one set of [.wasm] files.
+ Copies the wasm_of_ocaml loader ([<name>.bc.wasm.js]) and its
  [<name>.bc.wasm.assets] directory into the output directory.
+ Writes [load-wasm.js], which loads the precompiled wasm modules in workerd.
+ Writes [index.js], the worker entry point. It imports every [.wasm] file and
  exports the handlers that the program registered with {!Workero.serve} and
  {!Workero.scheduled}.

It prints [workero: wrote <dir> (<n> wasm file(s))]. On an error it prints
[workero: <reason>] to stderr and exits with code 1.

{1 Errors}

[workero build] and [workero package] exit with code 1 and print
[workero: <reason>] to stderr when:

- the arguments do not match the usage,
- [dune build] fails ([workero build] only),
- dune does not produce the loader or the [.wasm.assets] directory (the
  executable lacks [(modes wasm)], or the path is wrong),
- the target is outside the dune root ([workero build] only),
- the program uses OCaml functions that have no wasm implementation. The
  message is [workero: <name> uses functions with no wasm implementation:
  <list>]. They would fail at run time. Provide a wasm implementation with
  [(wasm_of_ocaml (wasm_files ...))], or drop the library that needs them.

The check also works for programs built with [--pretty] in [wasm_of_ocaml]
[flags].

{1 With Wrangler}

Point [main] at the generated [index.js] and run [workero build] as the build
command, so [wrangler dev] and [wrangler deploy] rebuild the worker:

{@toml[
main = "_worker/index.js"

[build]
command = "workero build src/main"
watch_dir = "src"
]}

wasm_of_ocaml needs [wasm-opt] from binaryen 119 or later on [PATH]. The npm
package [binaryen] provides it.
